# Line endings for files that are executed *inside* containers.
#
# backend/vayy/entrypoint.sh is run by /bin/sh (dash) in python:3.12-slim.
# With CRLF endings every invocation path breaks:
#   - "sh /app/entrypoint.sh"  -> line 2 becomes `set -e\r`
#                                -> dash: "set: Illegal option -" -> exit 2
#   - "/entrypoint.sh"         -> shebang becomes `/bin/sh\r`
#                                -> "bad interpreter" -> exit 126
#   - the `PYEOF` heredoc terminator never matches -> unterminated `if`
#
# This matters more than it looks: the Docker build context is the *worktree*,
# not the committed blob. On a Windows host with core.autocrlf=true, `git status`
# reports the file as clean while the worktree copy is CRLF, so the CRLF still
# ships into the image. `text eol=lf` pins both the worktree and the build
# context to LF regardless of each machine's autocrlf setting.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Compose files: the root docker-compose.yaml is checked out with CRLF on Windows
# hosts, which shows up as a phantom whole-file diff (every line "changed") and
# risks committing CRLF into the stack definition. YAML tolerates either ending,
# so normalise to LF. Scoped to compose files only.
docker-compose*.yaml text eol=lf
